Each additional year of schooling raises individual earnings by 8–13% (World Bank estimates); these returns aggregate to national productivity — countries with higher tertiary attainment have higher GDP per capita.
A 1% increase in the share of the workforce with tertiary education raises long-run GDP per capita by 0.5%, according to OECD economic modelling.
Germany's dual education and free university system produces the highest-skilled manufacturing workforce in the world, directly enabling the export-driven economic model that makes Germany the EU's largest economy.
Graduate workers pay significantly more in taxes over their lifetimes than the cost of their education subsidy — free university is a fiscal investment with a positive return, not a pure cost.
